-- title:
--   Hecke-self-adjoint pairings stay non-degenerate on idempotent corners
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M \ge 1$ with $M \neq 0$ as a type-class, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ and a set $S$ of natural numbers. Write $J_H =$ [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127) for the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent-series function field of $X_H$, and $T_p J_H$ for its Tate module, the group of sequences $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $J_H$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$. Let $\mathbb{T}$ be a commutative ring which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra acting on $T_p J_H$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action, let $op$ assign to each generator symbol $g$ in [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (a symbol $T_\ell$ for a prime $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, a symbol $U_q$ for a prime $q \mid M$, or a diamond symbol $\langle d \rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$) an element $op(g) \in \mathbb{T}$ whose action on $T_p J_H$ is the endomorphism [`ModularCurve.tateGenOpH M H S p g`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L99) induced by the corresponding Hecke or diamond operator [`ModularCurve.genOpH M H S g`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L80) on $J_H$, and assume the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{T}$ generated by the range of $op$ is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Let $e \in \mathbb{T}$ be idempotent and put $X$ for the image, under evaluation at index $1$, of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e \cdot T_p J_H$ (the range of $x \mapsto e \bullet x$). Let $b$ be a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bilinear form on the $p$-torsion subgroup $J_H[p] = \{x : p x = 0\}$ of $J_H$ which is non-degenerate on both sides ($b(x,-) = 0$ forces $x = 0$, and $b(-,y) = 0$ forces $y = 0$), and assume every generator is self-adjoint for $b$ in the following form: for each $g$ and all $x, y, x', y' \in J_H[p]$ with $x' =$ `genOpH M H S g` $x$ and $y' =$ `genOpH M H S g` $y$ in $J_H$, one has $b(x', y) = b(x, y')$. Then $b$ remains non-degenerate on both sides after restriction to $X$: if $x \in J_H[p]$ has image in $X$ and $b(x, y) = 0$ for every $y \in J_H[p]$ with image in $X$, then $x = 0$; and symmetrically in the second variable.
--
--   This is the orthogonality of Hecke idempotents for a Hecke-equivariant pairing on $J_H[p]$: since $J_H(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ is divisible, the idempotent $e$ descends to an idempotent on $J_H[p]$ with image $X$, which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-polynomial in the Hecke and diamond generators and hence self-adjoint, so the decomposition of $J_H[p]$ into the image of $e$ and its complement is $b$-orthogonal. It is used in the construction of idempotent-cut pieces of the $p$-adic geometry of $J_H$ at $p$, in particular for the perfect Hecke-equivariant pairings and the dual descriptions of differentials that feed the Taylor–Wiles argument.
